            {
              "id": "secret.razorpay_key_id",
              "name": "UseOfHardCodedCredentials",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hard-coded credential of type razorpay_key_id (masked preview rz****** (23 chars), entropy 4.26 bits/char)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Hard-coded credential of type razorpay_key_id (masked preview rz****** (23 chars), entropy 4.26 bits/char). A credential such as a password, API key or token is embedded in the source code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. Revoke and rotate the exposed credential pair immediately.\n2. Store the replacement values in the deployment platform's secrets manager; use protected environment variables only if a secrets manager is unavailable.\n3. Replace the hardcoded values in razorpayKeyId and razorpayKeySecret with runtime secret lookups.\n4. Validate both values at startup and fail closed when either is absent; do not add hardcoded fallback values.\n5. Remove the credentials from repository history, build caches, published packages, and deployment artifacts after rotation.\n6. Update each deployment environment with its own replacement credentials and verify authentication succeeds.\n\nSecure example:\n```javascript\nrazorpayKeyId: process.env.razorpayKeyId,\n  razorpayKeySecret: process.env.razorpayKeySecret,\n```\n\nDefence in depth:\n- Enable secret scanning in pre-commit hooks and CI to block future committed credentials.\n- Use separate, least-privileged credentials for each environment and rotate them periodically.\n- Prevent configuration objects and environment contents from being exposed through logs, diagnostics, or error responses.\n\nAdopt a secret manager, add pre-commit and CI secret scanning, and purge the value from version-control history.",
                "markdown": "**Fix now.** 1. Revoke and rotate the exposed credential pair immediately.\n2. Store the replacement values in the deployment platform's secrets manager; use protected environment variables only if a secrets manager is unavailable.\n3. Replace the hardcoded values in razorpayKeyId and razorpayKeySecret with runtime secret lookups.\n4. Validate both values at startup and fail closed when either is absent; do not add hardcoded fallback values.\n5. Remove the credentials from repository history, build caches, published packages, and deployment artifacts after rotation.\n6. Update each deployment environment with its own replacement credentials and verify authentication succeeds.\n\nSecure example:\n```javascript\nrazorpayKeyId: process.env.razorpayKeyId,\n  razorpayKeySecret: process.env.razorpayKeySecret,\n```\n\nDefence in depth:\n- Enable secret scanning in pre-commit hooks and CI to block future committed credentials.\n- Use separate, least-privileged credentials for each environment and rotate them periodically.\n- Prevent configuration objects and environment contents from being exposed through logs, diagnostics, or error responses.\n\n**Prevent recurrence.** Adopt a secret manager, add pre-commit and CI secret scanning, and purge the value from version-control history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
