            {
              "id": "py.command-injection",
              "name": "OsCommandInjection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A shell command is built from variable parts that are not quoted."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A shell command is built from variable parts that are not quoted. The application builds an operating-system command from data that an external party can influence and runs it through a shell. Shell metacharacters in that data change the command that is executed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. Replace the formatted command string with an argument list.\n2. Remove `shell=True`; `subprocess.run` then invokes the program without shell interpretation.\n3. Validate `archive_name` and `folder` against the narrow syntax required by the application, especially to prevent command-line option injection.\n4. Keep `check=True` so execution failures are not silently ignored.\n\nSecure example:\n```python\nsubprocess.run([\"zip\", \"-x\", archive_name, folder], check=True)\n```\n\nDefence in depth:\n- Allow-list each parameter according to its expected filename or pattern grammar, including rejecting unexpected option-like values such as those beginning with `-`.\n- Run the process with minimal filesystem permissions and access only to the required files.\n- Use a trusted executable path or tightly controlled `PATH`, and consider a timeout to constrain subprocess execution.\n\nReplace shell invocations with native library calls where possible, run the service with least privilege, and add a static-analysis gate that blocks shell=True and string-built commands.",
                "markdown": "**Fix now.** 1. Replace the formatted command string with an argument list.\n2. Remove `shell=True`; `subprocess.run` then invokes the program without shell interpretation.\n3. Validate `archive_name` and `folder` against the narrow syntax required by the application, especially to prevent command-line option injection.\n4. Keep `check=True` so execution failures are not silently ignored.\n\nSecure example:\n```python\nsubprocess.run([\"zip\", \"-x\", archive_name, folder], check=True)\n```\n\nDefence in depth:\n- Allow-list each parameter according to its expected filename or pattern grammar, including rejecting unexpected option-like values such as those beginning with `-`.\n- Run the process with minimal filesystem permissions and access only to the required files.\n- Use a trusted executable path or tightly controlled `PATH`, and consider a timeout to constrain subprocess execution.\n\n**Prevent recurrence.** Replace shell invocations with native library calls where possible, run the service with least privilege, and add a static-analysis gate that blocks shell=True and string-built commands."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-78",
                  "OWASP-A03:2021",
                  "security"
                ],
                "security-severity": "9.3",
                "precision": "medium"
