            {
              "id": "taint.http_param.html",
              "name": "CrossSiteScripting",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Data from a http_param source reaches a html sink (res.send)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Data from a http_param source reaches a html sink (res.send). Data from the request is written into an HTML response without context-aware output encoding, so a browser may interpret it as markup or script."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1. Use a maintained HTML-encoding library such as `escape-html`.\n2. Convert the query value to a string and encode it immediately before insertion into the HTML response.\n3. Keep the encoded value in HTML text content; use the encoder appropriate to the destination if it is later moved into an attribute, URL, script, or style context.\n4. If markup is not required, remove the HTML wrapper and explicitly send a `text/plain` response instead.\n5. Add regression tests using angle brackets, quotes, entities, and event-handler markup.\n\nSecure example:\n```javascript\nres.send(\n  '<xx>xxxxxxx xxxx, ' +\n  require('escape-html')(String(req.query.name ?? '')) +\n  '</xx>'\n);\n```\n\nDefence in depth:\n- Deploy a restrictive Content Security Policy, preferably without `unsafe-inline`, to reduce impact if another injection path exists.\n- Apply business-specific type and length limits to `req.query.name`; do not treat validation as a substitute for output encoding.\n- Prefer an auto-escaping server-side template engine for responses containing multiple dynamic HTML values.\n\nAdopt auto-escaping templates throughout, deploy a strict Content-Security-Policy, and set HttpOnly on session cookies.",
                "markdown": "**Fix now.** 1. Use a maintained HTML-encoding library such as `escape-html`.\n2. Convert the query value to a string and encode it immediately before insertion into the HTML response.\n3. Keep the encoded value in HTML text content; use the encoder appropriate to the destination if it is later moved into an attribute, URL, script, or style context.\n4. If markup is not required, remove the HTML wrapper and explicitly send a `text/plain` response instead.\n5. Add regression tests using angle brackets, quotes, entities, and event-handler markup.\n\nSecure example:\n```javascript\nres.send(\n  '<xx>xxxxxxx xxxx, ' +\n  require('escape-html')(String(req.query.name ?? '')) +\n  '</xx>'\n);\n```\n\nDefence in depth:\n- Deploy a restrictive Content Security Policy, preferably without `unsafe-inline`, to reduce impact if another injection path exists.\n- Apply business-specific type and length limits to `req.query.name`; do not treat validation as a substitute for output encoding.\n- Prefer an auto-escaping server-side template engine for responses containing multiple dynamic HTML values.\n\n**Prevent recurrence.** Adopt auto-escaping templates throughout, deploy a strict Content-Security-Policy, and set HttpOnly on session cookies."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
